We have just about 45 minutes.

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:14.650
Maybe, 44 minutes because you may have to
leave punctually at 1.

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:15.880
Am I correct about that?

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:23.066
So, I wanna, I wanna save most of the time
for

00:00:23.066 --> 00:00:31.678
your comments and questions to which I
will do my best to, to respond.

00:00:31.678 --> 00:00:37.760
[COUGH]
I wanna talk about three things briefly.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:42.280
I was asked to talk about leadership and I
will do so.

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:49.080
I wanna talk about the climate crisis and
the solutions to the climate crisis.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:51.760
You'd be surprised if I didn't.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:56.986
And then, I wanna say a few words on
sustainable capitalism, because as

00:00:56.986 --> 00:01:05.370
B-School students you are focused
naturally on the future of business.

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:09.730
And, I have strong views about the state
of business,

00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:12.920
the state of markets and the future of
business.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:15.360
First of all on leadership.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:17.480
I, thank you for inviting me to speak on
the topic.

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:23.266
I don't hold myself out as a, an expert on
leadership,

00:01:23.266 --> 00:01:30.898
I think that a based on my experience and
what I have tried to learn

00:01:30.898 --> 00:01:37.690
about leadership, I would start with the
saying by Loutse.

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:49.080
Who wrote of truly great leaders, that
people will say we did this ourselves.

00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:54.140
Worth reflection.
I have studied leadership,

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:58.910
I've had the privilege of being in a
number of positions where I

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:04.380
was called upon to provide leadership to
organizations and groups of men and women.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:11.740
And, there are obviously many different
styles of leadership,

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:15.570
and all I can do is speak to you about the
style and

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:18.930
approach that I think makes the most
sense.

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:25.570
We as human beings are each equipped with
our own

00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:30.810
independent ability to collect
information, process it

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:38.140
process the feelings and intuitions that
we have and

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:43.970
to make decisions e, individually and as a
part of groups.

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:48.630
Businesses are groups of people, and
success is,

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:53.420
at the risk of a cliche, a team sport.

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:59.990
And the leader of a team has to motivate
individuals,

00:02:59.990 --> 00:03:05.540
but has to be conscious of the reality of
the team.

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:07.320
How do you do that?

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:15.830
Let me give you a very arcane analogy
that's based on information technology.

00:03:15.830 --> 00:03:18.990
Here we are in Silicon Valley, so maybe
that'll be okay.

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:25.210
In the history of computing, the first
computers were based

00:03:25.210 --> 00:03:30.890
on central processing units surrounded by
fields of memory.

00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:37.237
And, as the memory fields grew, and the
processing power grew,

00:03:37.237 --> 00:03:42.600
limitations to that model soon appeared.

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:46.150
In structural terms,

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:52.340
the central processing unit would send out
to the field of memory, collect data,

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:57.830
bring it back to the center, process it,
and then store the answer.

00:03:59.510 --> 00:04:03.860
As the speed requirements advanced,

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:09.500
it became clear that this was a limitation
both in time and

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:14.470
in the generation of energy, which had
implications for

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:20.430
the size and materials, necessary and
design of computers.

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:25.392
IBM's first breakthrough was called vector
processing.

00:04:25.392 --> 00:04:29.510
It's initial success was really based on
that advance,

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:33.830
which simply meant that the central
processing unit, when sending out to

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:42.440
retrieve process and then distribute the
results of information processing.

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:48.100
One of these trips could begin while
others were in progress.

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:53.730
And so, that saved time, speed it up, the
computer, but

00:04:53.730 --> 00:05:01.330
all of the processing still had to take
place in the central processing unit.

00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:08.740
So, in the early in the 19 80s an
architectural design breakthrough

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:14.010
took place empowered by the relentless
advance of Morris Law,

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:17.320
as processors became smaller and more
powerful.

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:20.290
The central processing unit was broken up,

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:27.460
and smaller microprocessors were placed
throughout the field of memory.

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:32.420
And, with advances in software design, it
became possible to issue the machine

00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:38.380
a start order at which moment, all of the
information that needed

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:44.160
to be processed to produce the desired
result could be processed simultaneously

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:48.800
throughout the memory field, where it was
stored adjacent to microprocessors.

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:52.310
And then, the various fractions of the
answer could be

00:05:52.310 --> 00:05:54.040
brought to the center and assembled.

00:05:55.170 --> 00:06:00.330
So, instead of three trips, there was one
trip.

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:04.176
Faster, less heat energy generated,

00:06:04.176 --> 00:06:10.050
smaller machines, faster still, a virtuous
cycle.

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:16.720
So, what does that analogy have to say
about leadership in organizations?

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:24.010
If the leader of an organization insists
on being

00:06:24.010 --> 00:06:28.280
provided all of the information necessary
to make every single decision,

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:32.920
it is like the central processing unit
architecture.

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:40.080
And it is a cumbersome process generating
energy taking time.

00:06:42.110 --> 00:06:48.680
And, it does not maximize the assets of
the organization in the minds and

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:51.900
hears of the men and women who make up the
organization.

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:56.280
So, how do you make it possible for

00:06:56.280 --> 00:07:01.370
a human organization to process all of

00:07:01.370 --> 00:07:06.730
the parts of information, of the
information available to the organization.

00:07:06.730 --> 00:07:10.860
Simultaneously, in real time, and then
assemble the answers, so

00:07:10.860 --> 00:07:16.140
that the team as a whole can move swiftly
and effectively.

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:21.560
Well, to return to the analogy, in the
analogy of,

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:26.030
of the transition and computing design
from CPU base to

00:07:26.030 --> 00:07:31.880
massively parallel processing
architectures, the key is the software.

00:07:31.880 --> 00:07:35.440
I skipped right through that step very
quickly.

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:41.370
But obviously, in order for the start
order to trigger simultaneous

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:47.010
processing by multiple processors
throughout the memory field.

00:07:47.010 --> 00:07:47.760
And then,

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:53.000
direct the parts of the answer towards the
center, then assemble the answer.

00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:55.449
That requires very complex software.

00:07:56.580 --> 00:08:03.110
So, a leader in a human organization has
to do something analogues

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:09.540
to that software, and all simplifications
can conceal as much as they reveal.

00:08:09.540 --> 00:08:13.900
So, with that in mind, I'll just offer
this simplification,

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:17.290
which is hardly original with me, but,
and, you may recognize it.

00:08:17.290 --> 00:08:20.770
But, vision, values, and goals.

00:08:22.250 --> 00:08:27.680
The vision of the organization is central.

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:33.960
And the leader of any human organization
has to take primary responsiblity

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:38.340
for communicating that vision clearly.

00:08:38.340 --> 00:08:41.510
I say communicating, it has to be shared,
not only in words,

00:08:41.510 --> 00:08:47.770
not only in data, but emotionally,
spiritually.

00:08:47.770 --> 00:08:52.460
It has to be embodied in the way the
leader interacts with every

00:08:52.460 --> 00:08:54.280
part of the organization.

00:08:55.340 --> 00:08:58.040
Secondly, values are central.

00:08:58.040 --> 00:09:04.270
There has to be an agreement as broadly
and as tightly as possible among

00:09:04.270 --> 00:09:10.230
all the individuals in the organization on
values and what I mean by values,

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:17.560
it is not just what the, the word
instantly conveys, but, but

00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:23.520
but also I mean it in this sense, what
ethical and business criteria

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:29.650
are suppose to be used in making a
decision?

00:09:31.010 --> 00:09:35.780
If there is widespread agreement among all
of the individuals in

00:09:35.780 --> 00:09:40.680
the organization about those criteria,
about those values,

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:44.870
if you will, then it is more likely than
not

00:09:44.870 --> 00:09:52.000
that any individual in the organization
confronted with the same information,

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:58.410
empowered by a commitment to pursue the
same vision, will make a decision

00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:03.000
that is quite similar to what anyone else
in the organization would make.

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:10.200
So there is a congruency of vision, a
congruency of values, then goals.

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:17.240
There has to be a time ordered sequence
that reflects

00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:25.300
a sensible priority listing of what goals
are most important at any given time.

00:10:25.300 --> 00:10:31.590
And then of course, you know the old
cliche, you have to avoid at all cost,

00:10:31.590 --> 00:10:36.290
letting the urgent displace the important
because a sense of

00:10:36.290 --> 00:10:42.760
urgency is simply not the same as a sense
of priority.

00:10:42.760 --> 00:10:47.100
So the role of a leader then begins to be
clarified, at least in my mind.

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:52.490
The leader of an organization has to take
responsibility for

00:10:52.490 --> 00:10:55.610
constantly elevating the vision.

00:10:56.700 --> 00:11:01.170
A rekindling, a commitment to the vision.

00:11:01.170 --> 00:11:05.510
Reiterating constantly, exactly what the
vision is.

00:11:05.510 --> 00:11:08.690
What is the purpose of the organization?

00:11:08.690 --> 00:11:12.590
What are we trying to accomplish together?

00:11:12.590 --> 00:11:16.540
Secondly, the leader has to be responsible
for

00:11:16.540 --> 00:11:22.980
constantly reenforcing the values of the
organization.

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:26.710
Not brooking compromise on core values.

00:11:26.710 --> 00:11:30.640
And also translating those values into the
practical

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:35.590
criteria upon which decisions are supposed
to be based.

00:11:35.590 --> 00:11:43.290
And the leader third has to be responsible
for keeping track of the ever-changing

00:11:43.290 --> 00:11:50.600
list of priority goals and the sequence in
which they are supposed to be pursued.

00:11:50.600 --> 00:11:53.603
That to me is a formula that works,

00:11:53.603 --> 00:11:58.699
there're other models, there're other ways
to go about it, but

00:11:58.699 --> 00:12:04.620
for my part that's what I would like to
share with you about leadership.

00:12:05.820 --> 00:12:07.200
Now secondly.

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:08.340
The climate crisis.

00:12:08.340 --> 00:12:12.210
Perhaps you know everything you wanna know
about it.

00:12:12.210 --> 00:12:18.610
But if you are not personally thinking
about and engaged in the development of

00:12:18.610 --> 00:12:24.480
solutions for the climate crisis, then you
haven't thought clearly enough about it.

00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:30.270
Because by the time you are my age, it
will be crystal clear

00:12:31.460 --> 00:12:37.150
that the failures in the early part of the
21st Century to address climate

00:12:37.150 --> 00:12:43.990
change effectively were catastrophically
tragic.

00:12:43.990 --> 00:12:48.140
We still have time to avoid the,

00:12:48.140 --> 00:12:53.050
the worst unacceptable consequences of the
climate crisis.

00:12:53.050 --> 00:12:57.310
But, we have to get busy right away.

00:12:57.310 --> 00:13:03.670
and, and there is a a massive amount of
denial.

00:13:03.670 --> 00:13:06.930
You know the old cliche, denial ain't just
a river in Egypt.

00:13:06.930 --> 00:13:10.820
Along side that you could say disparaging
just a tyre in the trunk.

00:13:10.820 --> 00:13:14.920
There are people that move directly from

00:13:14.920 --> 00:13:20.420
denial right through the oh my god moment
to despair.

00:13:20.420 --> 00:13:25.380
Without pausing to actually solve the
problem that needs to be addressed.

00:13:25.380 --> 00:13:32.606
And it seems a forbidding task because the
scale of the challenge is so vast.

00:13:32.606 --> 00:13:41.340
In essence we are facing a completely new
reality on this planet.

00:13:41.340 --> 00:13:49.690
And it is so new and so unfamiliar that
the temptation is to label it implausible.

00:13:49.690 --> 00:13:53.060
Or at least, to search out for

00:13:53.060 --> 00:13:59.560
any acceptable excuse for doubting whether
it is real.

00:13:59.560 --> 00:14:05.720
And, and holding on to those doubts in
order to justify

00:14:05.720 --> 00:14:11.950
waiting before the, the time when it's
necessary to really focus on it.

00:14:13.150 --> 00:14:15.380
We don't have the luxury of time anymore.

00:14:15.380 --> 00:14:17.510
Here, here are the basic facts.

00:14:17.510 --> 00:14:21.466
Global warming is simply the largest and

00:14:21.466 --> 00:14:27.866
by far the most significant manifestation
of a deeper underlying

00:14:27.866 --> 00:14:34.748
collision between human civilization as it
is presently organized.

00:14:34.748 --> 00:14:40.879
And the ecological system of the planet
which has empowered human beings and

00:14:40.879 --> 00:14:45.115
nourished the flourishing of human
civilization.

00:14:45.115 --> 00:14:51.584
There have been three changes that have
coincided to create a radical

00:14:51.584 --> 00:14:59.174
discontinuity in the historical
relationship between the human species.

00:14:59.174 --> 00:15:00.940
And Planet Earth.

00:15:00.940 --> 00:15:03.165
Number one is population.

00:15:03.165 --> 00:15:08.789
I won't belabor this, but when the
scientists tell us that we first,

00:15:08.789 --> 00:15:15.044
emerged in, roughly, our modern form
approximately 200,000 years ago,

00:15:15.044 --> 00:15:20.259
midway between the second-to-last and
third-to-last Ice Age.

00:15:20.259 --> 00:15:23.514
We are a creature for all seasons.

00:15:23.514 --> 00:15:27.810
I'm going to resist the temptation to go
too deeply into any of these points.

00:15:27.810 --> 00:15:34.700
But, be that as it may, it has taken
200,000

00:15:34.700 --> 00:15:39.430
years before we reached the population of
one billion people.

00:15:40.660 --> 00:15:44.230
Sometime around the beginning on the 19th
century.

00:15:45.690 --> 00:15:50.810
We added that many people in the first 13
years of this century.

00:15:52.020 --> 00:15:56.190
We will add another billion in the next 14
years.

00:15:56.190 --> 00:15:59.930
And yet another billion 15 to 16 years
after that.

00:16:00.970 --> 00:16:07.440
We, we may stabilize global population at
nine or ten billion.

00:16:07.440 --> 00:16:13.320
There is a good deal of uncertainty about
the projections simply because the base

00:16:13.320 --> 00:16:18.670
is now so large that fractions of a child,
an odd phrase, I know.

00:16:18.670 --> 00:16:20.970
But fractions of a child.

00:16:20.970 --> 00:16:25.530
In the projections make the difference
between nine billion and

00:16:25.530 --> 00:16:31.410
14 billion or 18 billion we do know well,

00:16:31.410 --> 00:16:37.780
let me pause and say that this enormous
population explosion,

00:16:37.780 --> 00:16:42.890
they used to call it can be looked at in
retrospect.

00:16:42.890 --> 00:16:48.010
By noting that we have quadrupled human
population in less than 100 years.

00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:52.930
And that has contributed to this radical
change in

00:16:52.930 --> 00:16:56.860
the relationship between our species and
the planet.

00:16:56.860 --> 00:17:01.970
We could have many more people with a
completely different lifestyle,

00:17:01.970 --> 00:17:05.050
different technologies and a different
civilization, but

00:17:05.050 --> 00:17:09.340
given the stickiness of legacy
technologies and lifestyles.

00:17:09.340 --> 00:17:18.030
it, it is fair to assume that we really do
need to stabilize population.

00:17:18.030 --> 00:17:24.230
In that regard, we are witnessing a
success story unfolding in slow motion.

00:17:25.250 --> 00:17:29.490
Actually, we have seen the development in
the last quarter century.

00:17:29.490 --> 00:17:31.510
Of a very powerful and

00:17:31.510 --> 00:17:37.940
durable consensus worldwide on exactly how
to stabilize population.

00:17:37.940 --> 00:17:38.990
This is a success.

00:17:38.990 --> 00:17:43.630
I doesn't look like it but, but,
sometimes, but it is.

00:17:43.630 --> 00:17:45.280
It's a systems problem.

00:17:46.338 --> 00:17:50.885
Population dynamics is not a single cause,
single effect.

00:17:50.885 --> 00:17:55.799
Issue there are multiple factors involved,
but here is the distillation of

00:17:55.799 --> 00:18:01.343
what social scientists have, have, have
learned and proven in the field.

00:18:01.343 --> 00:18:06.454
There are four factors, all of which have
to be present, but

00:18:06.454 --> 00:18:11.580
when these four factors are present in any
society.

00:18:11.580 --> 00:18:17.760
Population changes from one equilibrium
state to another equilibrium state,

00:18:17.760 --> 00:18:18.520
it's an S curve.

00:18:19.760 --> 00:18:23.470
The first equilibrium state is high birth
rates,

00:18:24.820 --> 00:18:28.668
high death rates, and large families.

00:18:28.668 --> 00:18:34.280
Down the S-curve at the end of the
transition, the demographic transition,

00:18:34.280 --> 00:18:35.250
as they call it.

00:18:35.250 --> 00:18:37.500
There is a second equilibrium state.

00:18:38.550 --> 00:18:44.170
Small low birth rates, low death rates,
and small families.

00:18:45.290 --> 00:18:49.250
Small families are actually preferred by
people.

00:18:49.250 --> 00:18:53.840
During the agricultural revolution there
was a premium on having larger families.

00:18:53.840 --> 00:18:56.880
There are many other factors involved.

00:18:56.880 --> 00:19:00.410
But basically, the challenge has been how
to move

00:19:00.410 --> 00:19:05.480
societies around the world from one
equilibrium state to the other.

00:19:05.480 --> 00:19:07.270
That's where these four factors come in.

00:19:08.570 --> 00:19:10.090
Number one.

00:19:10.090 --> 00:19:11.370
The education of girls.

00:19:12.590 --> 00:19:15.440
Number two, the empowerment of women.

00:19:16.870 --> 00:19:23.090
Number three, the ubiquitous or nearly
ubiquitous availability

00:19:23.090 --> 00:19:28.130
of fertility management techniques and
technologies and knowledge.

00:19:29.310 --> 00:19:34.890
Number four, perhaps most significantly,
low child death rates.

00:19:35.940 --> 00:19:37.350
How does that last one fit in?

00:19:38.700 --> 00:19:44.290
If parents have confidence that their
children will survive into adulthood.

00:19:44.290 --> 00:19:47.810
And they have the ability to manage
fertility.

00:19:47.810 --> 00:19:50.920
They're going to want smaller families.

00:19:50.920 --> 00:19:55.470
If on the other hand, there is a high
child mortality rate,

00:19:55.470 --> 00:19:58.990
then mothers and fathers will want to

00:19:58.990 --> 00:20:05.010
have large families in order to ensure
that some of their children survive.

00:20:05.010 --> 00:20:09.200
Into adulthood, particularly in, in
societies where there are not,

00:20:10.380 --> 00:20:13.520
Social Security, type programs, and

00:20:13.520 --> 00:20:19.500
family, support is essential, in, in old
age.

00:20:19.500 --> 00:20:24.130
Now the good news is we made a lot of
progress in educating girls and

00:20:24.130 --> 00:20:25.450
empowering women.

00:20:25.450 --> 00:20:26.970
Not nearly enough.

00:20:26.970 --> 00:20:31.310
But we are on the right track all over the
world.

00:20:31.310 --> 00:20:36.560
We have also made a lot of progress in
reducing child death rates,

00:20:37.560 --> 00:20:39.900
really quite dramatic progress.

00:20:41.260 --> 00:20:46.710
Where we have lagged behind is making
fertility management available,

00:20:46.710 --> 00:20:49.920
particularly in places like Sub-Saharan
Africa,

00:20:49.920 --> 00:20:54.500
where the replacement rate is still much
higher than, than the global average.

00:20:54.500 --> 00:20:59.700
Africa, as a consequence midway through
this century, not long from now,

00:20:59.700 --> 00:21:02.490
will have more people than either China or
India.

00:21:02.490 --> 00:21:06.940
And at the end of this century, will have
more people than China and India combined.

00:21:08.160 --> 00:21:13.860
But in much of the world, this transition,
the demographic transition has taken place

00:21:13.860 --> 00:21:20.330
in all the developed countries, North
America, Europe, Japan, et cetera.

00:21:20.330 --> 00:21:21.860
Even in in, in China.

00:21:23.640 --> 00:21:26.610
Brazil, the BRICs, the emerging economies.

00:21:26.610 --> 00:21:27.310
They're, they're all.

00:21:27.310 --> 00:21:28.170
Mexico.

00:21:28.170 --> 00:21:32.330
They, they, they are all coming down this
S curve.

00:21:34.860 --> 00:21:40.370
So this is a good news, it's a, a positive
development.

00:21:40.370 --> 00:21:46.080
But those that have, that, that are not
getting fertility management, have a right

00:21:46.080 --> 00:21:51.065
to expect that wealthy developed
countries, principally the United States.

00:21:51.065 --> 00:21:55.950
Will actually follow through on the
pledges made to help them

00:21:55.950 --> 00:22:00.510
get access to fertility management, this
is the, the one problem there.

00:22:00.510 --> 00:22:04.930
Anyway, that's the first factor, even
though it's a success story, the large

00:22:04.930 --> 00:22:08.730
increase in population has changed the
relationship we have to the planet.

00:22:08.730 --> 00:22:14.610
Second, and far more significant, is the
technology revolution

00:22:14.610 --> 00:22:20.020
that took a turn 150 years ago, after the
first Industrial Revolution.

00:22:20.020 --> 00:22:24.100
Powered by coal, the first oil well was
drilled a century and half ago.

00:22:24.100 --> 00:22:27.410
And in the 20th century, the combination
of oil and

00:22:27.410 --> 00:22:31.858
coal became the basis of energy in the
world.

00:22:31.858 --> 00:22:37.550
And today, 85% of the energy used by Earth
Inc,

00:22:37.550 --> 00:22:41.420
the global economy, comes from fossil
fuels.

00:22:41.420 --> 00:22:47.800
And as a result, we now put 90 million
tons everyday of heat trapping,

00:22:47.800 --> 00:22:53.000
global warming pollution that's dumped in
to the atmosphere every 24 hours.

00:22:53.000 --> 00:22:55.360
As if the atmosphere is an open sewer.

00:22:56.520 --> 00:22:59.970
You know, in the 19th century a, a great
doctor in England

00:23:01.110 --> 00:23:05.270
was dealing with a cholera epidemic, which
used to be one of the biggest killers.

00:23:05.270 --> 00:23:09.740
And he innovated a location based data
system and

00:23:09.740 --> 00:23:13.300
mapped all of the cases of cholera in
London.

00:23:13.300 --> 00:23:18.470
And overlaid it on the sewer system and
found that a single malfunctioning pump on

00:23:18.470 --> 00:23:23.410
Broad Street was the proximate cause of
the cholera epidemic.

00:23:23.410 --> 00:23:27.460
Not long after that Louis Pasteur
discovered the bacteria that was

00:23:27.460 --> 00:23:29.830
responsible for cholera, and

00:23:29.830 --> 00:23:34.640
the, it is no longer the, the major cause
of death in the world that it used to be.

00:23:34.640 --> 00:23:39.800
He connected the dots, an, and it traced
to the sewer system.

00:23:39.800 --> 00:23:46.250
We have to connect the dots between the 90
million tons of global warming pollution

00:23:46.250 --> 00:23:51.960
spewed into the atmosphere every day, the
decision to use the atmosphere as a sewer,

00:23:51.960 --> 00:23:57.890
and the, and the resulting consequences of
all that extra trapped heat.

00:23:58.930 --> 00:24:03.170
We're raising the temperature of the
planet.

00:24:03.170 --> 00:24:09.220
In fact, the accumulated man-made global
warming pollution now traps as much

00:24:09.220 --> 00:24:14.710
extra energy in, in the, in the atmosphere
every day as would be released

00:24:14.710 --> 00:24:21.200
by 400,000 Hiroshima-style atomic bombs
every single day.

00:24:21.200 --> 00:24:23.980
It's a big planet, but that's a lot of
energy, and

00:24:23.980 --> 00:24:28.890
that's what evaporating much more water
vapor from the oceans into the warmer air

00:24:28.890 --> 00:24:32.570
of the atmosphere, which has an increased
holding power.

00:24:32.570 --> 00:24:34.840
Holding capacity for water vapor.

00:24:34.840 --> 00:24:38.500
So when storm conditions release a
downpour,

00:24:38.500 --> 00:24:41.020
the storms reach out 2,000 kilometers off
and

00:24:41.020 --> 00:24:46.450
then funnel these rivers of water vapor in
the sky into these massive downpours

00:24:46.450 --> 00:24:50.370
that are breaking records all over the
world, creating these floods.

00:24:50.370 --> 00:24:53.652
The same extra heat is pulling the
moisture out of

00:24:53.652 --> 00:24:59.350
the soil making massive droughts much more
widespread and much more frequent.

00:24:59.350 --> 00:25:03.870
The extra heat in the oceans is powering
much stronger wind speeds,

00:25:03.870 --> 00:25:08.231
which makes the storms more destructive,
melting the ice,

00:25:08.231 --> 00:25:12.453
50% of the Arctic ice cap is now gone, as
of last September.

00:25:12.453 --> 00:25:16.154
All of it will be gone in the next 10, 20
years.

00:25:16.154 --> 00:25:23.125
And that's changing the pattern of the jet
stream, and the storm tracks.

00:25:23.125 --> 00:25:29.456
It is disrupting the age, the timing of
precipitation events and

00:25:29.456 --> 00:25:36.247
the clockwork of rainy season, dry season
alternation in,

00:25:36.247 --> 00:25:44.950
in developing countries disrupting
sustainable agriculture.

00:25:44.950 --> 00:25:45.740
Not sustainable.

00:25:45.740 --> 00:25:51.820
Subsistence agriculture in countries where
that is the principal occupation.

00:25:51.820 --> 00:25:53.750
The melting ice raises sea level.

00:25:53.750 --> 00:25:55.580
You know the rest.

00:25:55.580 --> 00:26:00.950
Moves disease vectors from the tropics and
sub-tropics into the temperate zones.

00:26:00.950 --> 00:26:04.530
And the list of horribles is actually much
longer than that.

00:26:05.990 --> 00:26:09.370
The tendency in the face of such evidence
is to turn away from it.

00:26:10.500 --> 00:26:14.650
And that is, that leads to the third cause
after, population and

00:26:14.650 --> 00:26:17.360
technology, and that is our way of
thinking.

00:26:18.460 --> 00:26:21.390
We are empowered with thought patterns and

00:26:21.390 --> 00:26:27.130
habits that we have inherited from our
ancient ancestors.

00:26:27.130 --> 00:26:31.910
And as has often been the case in the
history of civilization,

00:26:31.910 --> 00:26:36.250
our real challenge is to accelerate the
emergence.

00:26:36.250 --> 00:26:41.460
Of wisdom that can be used to deal with
new circumstances.

00:26:41.460 --> 00:26:46.970
We're built for that, we are creatures of
habit we, we do

00:26:46.970 --> 00:26:53.250
manifest the legacies of our evolutionary
paths but, we also have the capacity

00:26:53.250 --> 00:27:00.920
to rise above our limitations and to see
clearly the changes that need to be made.

00:27:00.920 --> 00:27:07.200
One of the things that needs to change is
our vulnerability to short term thinking.

00:27:07.200 --> 00:27:13.990
Now our present culture in business, in
media, in politics, in almost every

00:27:13.990 --> 00:27:19.640
realm of life has now magnified this
tendency to focus on the short term.

00:27:19.640 --> 00:27:23.200
And made it a very serious problem.

00:27:23.200 --> 00:27:26.798
So let me use that as a segue to the third
topic that I'll cover very briefly, and

00:27:26.798 --> 00:27:28.957
then open it up to your questions and
comments.

00:27:28.957 --> 00:27:32.251
Sustainable capitalism.

00:27:32.251 --> 00:27:40.390
Short-termism, you, you no doubt have
already identified that as a buzzword,.

00:27:40.390 --> 00:27:43.710
It is an extremely serious problem.

00:27:45.030 --> 00:27:50.300
One of our leading business research
institutions BNA, conducted a famous study

00:27:50.300 --> 00:27:55.980
a few years ago in which they asked CEOs
and CFOs a whole series of questions.

00:27:55.980 --> 00:27:57.160
One of them was a hypothetical.

00:27:58.300 --> 00:28:02.130
Here's a, an investment you can make in
your business, the question began.

00:28:03.210 --> 00:28:04.590
If you make this investment,

00:28:04.590 --> 00:28:09.490
you're business will become more
profitable, stronger, more sustainable,

00:28:09.490 --> 00:28:14.990
more resilient, it meets your internal
investment targets.

00:28:14.990 --> 00:28:16.750
But there's one, issue.

00:28:16.750 --> 00:28:18.660
If you make this investment,

00:28:18.660 --> 00:28:24.140
you will slightly miss your next quarter's
earnings projection.

00:28:25.490 --> 00:28:28.090
Given those facts, will you make the
investment?

00:28:29.220 --> 00:28:30.960
Eighty percent said no.

00:28:32.680 --> 00:28:35.025
That is functionally insane.

00:28:35.025 --> 00:28:38.360
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It truly is.

00:28:38.360 --> 00:28:41.340
Because it hurts the shareholders.

00:28:41.340 --> 00:28:43.460
It hurts the employees.

00:28:43.460 --> 00:28:48.050
it, it hurts the communities where the
business is operating.

00:28:48.050 --> 00:28:52.070
But it, it is now a routine decision.

00:28:53.080 --> 00:28:58.070
Because, of course missing a quarterly
earnings projection

00:28:58.070 --> 00:29:02.540
leads to a reduction in market
capitalization, and

00:29:02.540 --> 00:29:08.610
whatever benefits come from focusing on
the long term build up of sustainable

00:29:08.610 --> 00:29:14.200
value, could, can be wiped out given the
way financing markets now operate.

00:29:15.640 --> 00:29:19.460
so, what do we need to do to reform
capitalism?

00:29:19.460 --> 00:29:21.260
Let me first say, it,

00:29:21.260 --> 00:29:25.270
it hardly needs to be said particularly in
this group, but capitalism's,

00:29:25.270 --> 00:29:31.632
capitalism is the best way of organizing
economic activity ever created.

00:29:31.632 --> 00:29:35.798
It allocates resources efficiently,
balances supply and

00:29:35.798 --> 00:29:41.477
demand is more congruent with higher
levels of freedom in human societies.

00:29:41.477 --> 00:29:43.937
And perhaps, most significantly,

00:29:43.937 --> 00:29:48.153
capitalism unlocks a higher fraction of
the human potential.

00:29:48.153 --> 00:29:54.062
With a ubiquitous set of organic
incentives that routinely

00:29:54.062 --> 00:30:01.400
reward hard work, initiative, creativity
and, and innovation.

00:30:01.400 --> 00:30:04.305
&gt;&gt; But in spite of those well documented
strengths,

00:30:04.305 --> 00:30:08.869
which had made capitalism the base of
every successful economy in the world and

00:30:08.869 --> 00:30:12.430
there are different varieties of
capitalism.

00:30:12.430 --> 00:30:14.870
But in essence, it, it, it is ubiquitous
now.

00:30:15.980 --> 00:30:17.790
But in spite of those strengths,

00:30:17.790 --> 00:30:22.630
capitalism is increasingly being
associated with a series of problems.

00:30:23.670 --> 00:30:24.790
Growing inequality.

00:30:26.760 --> 00:30:32.620
Almost everywhere in the world, the U.S.,
Europe, Japan, China, India Brazil.

00:30:32.620 --> 00:30:39.080
Almost everywhere in the world, inequality
is growing beyond the normal levels.

00:30:39.080 --> 00:30:41.070
Inequality is endemic to capitalism.

00:30:41.070 --> 00:30:43.290
It's good, we should have inequality.

00:30:43.290 --> 00:30:48.820
That's part of the des, that's part of why
this system of incentives works.

00:30:48.820 --> 00:30:55.070
But, as with inflation, if you go from a
normal level to a hyper level.

00:30:55.070 --> 00:30:58.290
Hyper inflation soaks up so much energy
and

00:30:58.290 --> 00:31:03.640
so many resources that the, the normal
functioning comes almost to a halt.

00:31:03.640 --> 00:31:08.240
Hyper inequality is threatening to both
ca, both to capitalism and democracy.

00:31:09.370 --> 00:31:17.300
Since the, recovery, such as it is, began
after the recent great recession, 93%

00:31:17.300 --> 00:31:23.020
of the additional, income in the United
States has indeed gone to the top 1%.

00:31:23.020 --> 00:31:26.010
That's not an Occupy Wall Street slogan.

00:31:26.010 --> 00:31:28.260
That's a fact.

00:31:28.260 --> 00:31:34.920
And while these flows of income have been
channelled to the top, middle

00:31:34.920 --> 00:31:39.610
income families have been struggling and
are increasingly struggling.

00:31:39.610 --> 00:31:41.790
Okay.
This is not a political polemic,

00:31:41.790 --> 00:31:45.260
it is an observation, and the consequences
of this,

00:31:45.260 --> 00:31:49.300
again, are threatening to democratic
capitalism.

00:31:49.300 --> 00:31:50.170
What else?

00:31:50.170 --> 00:31:55.190
A series of disruptions in the global
economy,

00:31:55.190 --> 00:31:59.808
occurring with greater frequency and at
higher magnitudes.

00:31:59.808 --> 00:32:07.630
Missile-lined incentives, where not, not
just principal agent problems.

00:32:07.630 --> 00:32:14.380
But misaligned incentives that lead to, to
allocation, al, decisions and

00:32:14.380 --> 00:32:20.520
investment decisions that again don't make
good rational sense.

00:32:20.520 --> 00:32:25.640
These problems need to be addressed on
increasing focus on the short term,

00:32:25.640 --> 00:32:26.520
as I said earlier.

00:32:26.520 --> 00:32:27.440
So what should we do?

00:32:29.300 --> 00:32:32.530
I think that there need to be some

00:32:32.530 --> 00:32:39.500
significant reforms in the design of
markets and in the design of capitalism.

00:32:39.500 --> 00:32:43.070
Since this ubiquitous set of organic
incentives is the key to

00:32:43.070 --> 00:32:46.270
unlocking the energy in markets.

00:32:46.270 --> 00:32:49.620
We need to pay careful attention to what
the incentives are.

00:32:49.620 --> 00:32:52.400
People will often do what you pay them to
do.

00:32:52.400 --> 00:32:55.850
And if you pay them to focus on the short
term, they will do that.

00:32:55.850 --> 00:32:59.110
Even if you also signal to them that you
want them to tell you that

00:32:59.110 --> 00:33:01.780
they're focused on the long term, if you
reward them and

00:33:01.780 --> 00:33:05.700
pay them al, to focus on the short term,
they will do that.

00:33:08.060 --> 00:33:09.310
30 years ago in the U.S.

00:33:09.310 --> 00:33:14.670
the average holding period for public
equities was roughly seven years.

00:33:14.670 --> 00:33:18.100
Which is congruent with the buildup of
real value in firms.

00:33:18.100 --> 00:33:20.740
Now it's less than six months.

00:33:21.830 --> 00:33:26.060
And on both of the New York and London
exchange is more than half of the trades

00:33:26.060 --> 00:33:32.440
are now made in, by algorithmic computer
trading in, in milliseconds.

00:33:32.440 --> 00:33:39.400
So, we need to get rid of this idea that
more liquidity is always better.

00:33:39.400 --> 00:33:41.860
We do not have perfect information.

00:33:41.860 --> 00:33:45.500
Markets do not tend toward equilibrium.

00:33:46.580 --> 00:33:53.550
and, and we need to understand that the
standard model is now obsolete.

00:33:53.550 --> 00:33:55.960
What will the new model be, I don't know.

00:33:55.960 --> 00:33:58.270
A lot of people think behavioral economics
and

00:33:58.270 --> 00:34:01.290
behavioral investing is the leading
candidate.

00:34:01.290 --> 00:34:04.680
I kinda think that, but it's above my pay
grade.

00:34:04.680 --> 00:34:06.630
So I will let you all figure that out.

00:34:06.630 --> 00:34:10.749
Let me conclude, and then open it up to
you.

00:34:11.990 --> 00:34:15.110
By telling you, just, just one brief
story.

00:34:15.110 --> 00:34:18.650
Because the climate crisis can seem
daunting.

00:34:19.820 --> 00:34:24.300
The challenge of fixing capitalism can
seem daunting.

00:34:24.300 --> 00:34:29.180
But look, look, things can change very
quickly.

00:34:29.180 --> 00:34:34.620
I remember as a, a child, as a young guy,
and growing up in Tennessee.

00:34:34.620 --> 00:34:38.120
When the civil rights revolution swept the
country.

00:34:38.120 --> 00:34:39.890
I remember when the conversation changed.

00:34:39.890 --> 00:34:42.420
Somebody made some racist comment in my
circle, and

00:34:42.420 --> 00:34:44.550
somebody else said, nah, we don't go for
that.

00:34:44.550 --> 00:34:47.450
And it, in, in millions of conversations
it changed.

00:34:48.820 --> 00:34:53.380
Two months ago, saw an article about two
gay men waiting in a long line for pizza.

00:34:53.380 --> 00:34:57.360
Holding hands and some homophobe walked
by, made a nasty comment.

00:34:57.360 --> 00:35:00.590
Literally every other person in that line
turned on the homophobe and

00:35:00.590 --> 00:35:01.920
said hey, shut up.

00:35:01.920 --> 00:35:03.400
We don't go for that anymore.

00:35:03.400 --> 00:35:05.910
We're winning that conversation.

00:35:05.910 --> 00:35:10.120
When the culture shifts, when
conscientiousness changes,

00:35:10.120 --> 00:35:13.240
then everything else can change rapidly.

00:35:13.240 --> 00:35:17.340
You may have noticed, countries all around
the world are approving gay marriage,

00:35:17.340 --> 00:35:20.620
getting rid of the discrimination against
gays and lesbians.

00:35:20.620 --> 00:35:24.480
If somebody had asked in your age group,
you wouldn't have been surprised at all,

00:35:24.480 --> 00:35:27.470
but in my age group, if somebody had said
five years ago we would see

00:35:27.470 --> 00:35:31.490
this revolutionary change, most people
would have said, nah, can't do it.

00:35:31.490 --> 00:35:35.080
We're doing it, because consciousness
changed.

00:35:35.080 --> 00:35:39.900
That's what has to happen with the
conversation about global warming and

00:35:39.900 --> 00:35:41.880
sustainable capitalism.

00:35:41.880 --> 00:35:43.190
Now finally.

00:35:43.190 --> 00:35:48.180
I remember as a boy of 13 when President
Kennedy issued the challenge to put

00:35:48.180 --> 00:35:51.540
a person on the moon and bring him back
safely in 10 years.

00:35:51.540 --> 00:35:54.800
And I remember hearing so many say that's
a mistake, we can't do that.

00:35:54.800 --> 00:35:58.940
But eight years and two months later, Neil
Armstrong set foot on the moon.

00:35:58.940 --> 00:36:00.040
And the moment he did,

00:36:00.040 --> 00:36:05.540
so there was a great cheer that went up in
NASA's mission control in Houston.

00:36:05.540 --> 00:36:07.990
The average age of those systems engineers
was 26.

00:36:07.990 --> 00:36:12.750
Which meant when they heard that
challenge, they were 18.

00:36:12.750 --> 00:36:18.210
I know that some of you have come here to
this great institution.

00:36:18.210 --> 00:36:22.480
To be of, a part of something larger than
yourself.

00:36:22.480 --> 00:36:27.800
I would end by challenging all of you to
aim in that direction.

00:36:27.800 --> 00:36:34.640
These are times when it is a privilege to
have the opportunity to do good

00:36:34.640 --> 00:36:40.330
things that what will be looked back upon
as a turning point for human civilization.

00:36:40.330 --> 00:36:45.570
And business is at the center of the
change that has to take place.

00:36:45.570 --> 00:36:47.270
So, we need the will to act, but

00:36:47.270 --> 00:36:51.640
always remember that the will to act is
itself a renewable resource.

00:36:51.640 --> 00:36:52.490
Thank you very much.

00:36:52.490 --> 00:36:54.365
I will open it up to your questions now.

